    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>

<p>The function is
\(f(x)=2\tan\left(x-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)+1\).</p>

<p>The standard tangent function \(y=\tan x\) has period \(\pi\), vertical asymptotes at \(x=\frac{\pi}{2}+n\pi\), and its central point at \((0,0)\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Vertical scale factor</strong></p>

<p>The coefficient of the tangent function is \(2\). Therefore, the graph is stretched vertically by a factor of \(2\).</p>


<p>The correct answer is \(\displaystyle 2\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Horizontal translation</strong></p>

<p>The expression \(x-\frac{\pi}{4}\) indicates a translation to the right by \(\frac{\pi}{4}\).</p>


<p>The correct value is \(\displaystyle h=\frac{\pi}{4}\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Vertical translation</strong></p>

<p>The constant \(+1\) outside the tangent function translates the graph upwards by \(1\) unit.</p>


<p>The correct answer is \(\displaystyle k=1\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Vertical asymptotes</strong></p>

<p>For \(f(x)=a\tan(x-h)+k\), the vertical asymptotes occur where
\(x-h=\frac{\pi}{2}+n\pi\), so
\(x=h+\frac{\pi}{2}+n\pi\).</p>

<p>With \(h=\frac{\pi}{4}\), the two asymptotes nearest the origin are
\(\displaystyle x=-\frac{\pi}{4}\) and
\(\displaystyle x=\frac{3\pi}{4}\).</p>



<p><strong>Step 5: Centre point</strong></p>

<p>The centre point of the middle branch is translated from \((0,0)\) to \((h,k)\). Therefore, the centre is
\(\displaystyle \left(\frac{\pi}{4},1\right)\).</p>



<p><strong>Summary</strong></p>

<p>The graph has vertical scale factor \(2\), horizontal translation \(\frac{\pi}{4}\) to the right, and vertical translation \(1\) unit upwards. Its nearest vertical asymptotes are \(x=-\frac{\pi}{4}\) and \(x=\frac{3\pi}{4}\), and the centre of the middle branch is \(\left(\frac{\pi}{4},1\right)\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
